:title("Go (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses a Go subset and executes it directly, without generating code.

The subset: package and import clauses (parsed, ignored), functions with typed
parameters and multiple return values (func divmod(a int, b int) (int, int)), methods
with value receivers (func (p Point) manhattan() int), struct types with positional
composite literals (Point{3, 4}), field access and assignment, := short declarations
(also multiple: q, r := divmod(7, 2)), var declarations with zero values, parallel
assignment (a, b = b, a) and the blank identifier _, slices ([]int{...}, append, len,
indexing, element assignment), maps (map[K]V{...} literals and make(map[K]V); reads
of missing keys yield the zero value of the value type - int/string/bool -, m[k] = v,
len, delete(m, k), the comma ok idiom v, ok := m[k], and range over the entries),
function literals as values (func(x int) int { ... }) with real closures over their
scope, defer for plain and pkg.fn calls (arguments evaluate at defer time, the calls
run LIFO at function exit), the three for forms (classic, condition only, and
for i, v := range s - also over an int like range 5, Go 1.22, and over maps), switch
with subject
(case 2, 3: compares ==) and without (the first true case wins; no fallthrough, an
implicit break ends every case), Go style if/else without parentheses (else if works), break,
continue, i++ and i-- as statements, assignment operators += -= *= /= %=, fmt.Println
and fmt.Print, os.Exit, and string concatenation. int arithmetic is 32 bit, / truncates.

Also genuinely implemented (added while widening toward the full language, and
measured by tests/go-test-full.go): grouped declarations - several names sharing a
type in parameters, results, struct fields, var and const specs -, named results
with a bare return, variadic parameters and the s... spread, the full operator set
(bitwise and or xor and-not, shifts, the matching compound assignments, unary
complement), const blocks with iota and implicit repetition, every composite-literal
form (slice, array with counted or indexed elements, map, struct - positional or
keyed -, anonymous struct types, and untyped nested brace groups), make for maps and
slices, new, the conversion builtins, cap/copy/clear/min/max/append with many values,
call suffixes on any expression (func literals, elements, returned functions), slice
expressions, if and switch with an init statement, fallthrough, labelled statements
with labelled break and continue and goto, per-iteration loop variables (Go 1.22),
defer on any callee including function literals, interface declarations (dispatch is
dynamic, so satisfaction is structural by construction), type switches, the two-result
type assertion, value versus pointer method receivers, method values, embedded structs
with field promotion, and panic/recover with the defers of every unwound frame.

Widened surface (parsed so a real Go file gets through; -warn-unsupported downgrades
the abort to a warning and keeps the rest running, enough for call graphs/CFGs/traces):
channel and chan types are parsed in type positions. Accepted but reported not
implemented (no concurrency or channel model): goroutines (go f()), channel send
(ch <- v) and receive (<-ch), and select. Where operands exist (the goroutine callee,
the send/receive channel and value) they still evaluate under -warn-unsupported so
their calls stay visible.

Go strings are BYTE strings here too: len and s[i] count UTF-8 bytes, a string slice
s[lo:hi] is a byte range, ranging a string yields byte offsets and runes, and the
literal escapes are Go's own (the byte-wise x and octal forms, and the eight digit U
form).

Deviations and limits: structs behave like references (as if every value were a *T),
append mutates in place (write s = append(s, v) as usual), a bare-name composite
literal must not appear in an if/for/switch header (the bracketed forms may), map
iteration and printing follow the insertion order (real Go randomizes resp. sorts),
'var m map[K]V' is not a usable map (create maps with a literal or make). There is one
numeric type at RUNTIME, so a float and an int with the same value are
indistinguishable and integer division decides - except where a written-out conversion
makes the type known while the program is built: float64(i)/2 is 2.5 and a sized
unsigned type wraps to its width, so uint8(0xF0) complements to 0x0F. A pointer to a
plain variable IS modelled (and so is
new(T) of a scalar): it is a cell over the variable's scope slot, so *p = v, f(&n) and
**pp = v write through and p == &n holds. Every other &-form is the identity on the
value, since structs, slices and maps behave like references already.

An ARRAY is a plain host array and a SLICE is a header {a, o, n, c} over one, so the
two behave as Go says: an array is a VALUE (assigning it, or passing it, copies, and
the copy is deep), while s[1:3] shares the backing array, cap() answers something of
its own, s[:2:3] caps the capacity and append writes into the shared storage while it
fits. complex128 is a {re, im} box entered through an imaginary literal or complex().

UNTYPED CONSTANTS are evaluated EXACTLY at compile time and rounded ONCE, where the
value reaches a typed location, which is Go's rule and not an optimisation: 0.1 + 0.2
is the double nearest three tenths (0.3), (1 << 100) >> 98 is 4, 2.0/3.0 !=
0.6666666666666666 even though both round to the same double, and a complex constant
folds as a PAIR of exact rationals. Literals, NAMED constants and expressions over
them all fold; the front end carries its own parse-time scope for the names, and
DECLINES - falling back to ordinary runtime arithmetic - wherever it cannot prove the
name is that constant: a spec with a written-out TYPE (which Go rounds at the
declaration), anything in a const group mentioning iota or repeating the previous
spec's expressions, a name used earlier in the file than its declaration, and a name
that any other binder (a var, :=, a parameter, a receiver, a named result, a range or
type-switch variable, an assignment target, a func, a type, an import alias) owns
where it is used. A written-out conversion is Go's own reason not to fold, so
float64(0.1) + float64(0.2) really is 0.30000000000000004. complex(0.1, 0.2) is a
constant expression in Go and is NOT folded here (the builtin call is not a fold
site), and division or remainder by zero, a shift count over 4096 and an intermediate
wider than 700 limbs each abandon the fold - all four are compile errors or
unrepresentable in real Go anyway.

Generics are ERASED: type parameter
lists, constraints and instantiations (f[int], T[K, V]) are parsed and ignored, which
behaves like the instantiated form in a dynamically typed value model.

The entry point is func main(); self checking programs end with os.Exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     = PackageClause { Import } { TypeDecl | TypeGroup | TopConst | FuncDecl | TopVar } ;

PackageClause = "package" KwEnd Id [ ";" ] ;
// An import spec may carry a local name: an alias (import f "fmt"), the blank
// identifier (import _ "x", imported for its side effects only) or a dot
// (import . "fmt", which drops the package's exported names into file scope).
// The alias really binds - globalScope[alias] is aimed at the same package
// object the base name would get - so f.Println works; the dot form is parsed
// and reported not implemented, because file-scope injection has no model here.
Import      = "import" KwEnd ( ImportSpec | "(" { ImportSpec } ")" ) [ ";" ] ;
ImportSpec  = ImportDot | ImportNamed | ImportPlain ;
ImportPlain <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~> = ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportNamed <~~ importAs(takeAll(), up.pos) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportDot   <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos); notImpl("dot import", up.pos) ~~>
            = "." ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportPath  <~~ push(up.in.slice(1, up.in.length - 1)) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7e" } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// type Point struct { x int; y int } and type Shape interface { Area() int }.
// The name is pushed first; the body decides between a struct (built) and an
// interface (accepted, then reported not implemented - no interface semantics).
// `type A = B` is an ALIAS: A and B name the same type rather than two distinct ones,
// so the alias is bound to whatever B is already bound to (a struct descriptor stays a
// struct descriptor, which a defined type's conversion wrapper would have destroyed).
// The trailing ';' is the pre-1.0 spelling the corpus is full of.
TypeDecl    <~~ buildTypeDecl(takeAll(), up.pos) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeParams ] [ AliasEq ] ( StructBody | IfaceBody | DefinedType ) [ ";" ] ;
AliasEq     <~~ push({alias: true}) ~~> = "=" ;
// type myInt int / type Celsius float64: a defined type over an existing one. The
// value model has no distinct type for it, so the name becomes the CONVERSION to its
// underlying type - which is exactly how a defined type is used in an expression.
DefinedType <~~ push({under: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// Struct fields: one or more names sharing a type (x, y int), an optional raw
// string tag, and embedded fields (a bare type name, whose field name is the type).
StructBody  = "struct" KwEnd "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl   = NamedField | EmbedField ;
NamedField  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } SameLine FType [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FType       <~~ push({fty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// An embedded field's NAME is its type name (w.base09 as well as the promoted w.id).
EmbedField  = [ "*" ] [ Id "." ] Id <~~ push(up.in); push({fty: ""}) ~~> [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FieldTag    = RawString <~~ pop() ~~> ;
// THE METHOD SET IS PARSED WHEN THE BODY IS AN ORDINARY METHOD LIST, and the
// SkipBlock alternative behind it keeps every other interface body parsing exactly as
// it did: a constraint interface (~int | ~string), an embedded interface name, a
// commented body all fail IfaceMethods and fall through with NO method names, which
// means "satisfied by anything" - the same answer this grammar gave before. up.in is
// not usable for this: a captured type text arrives with its WHITESPACE ALREADY
// STRIPPED, so `Speak() string Fly()` reads as one identifier `stringFly`.
// docs/todo.md 1.5.
IfaceBody   <~~ push({iface: true, imeth: cIfaceMeths(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "interface" KwEnd ( IfaceMethods | SkipBlock ) ;
IfaceMethods = "{" { IfaceMeth } "}" ;
IfaceMeth   = Id <~~ push({imname: up.in}) ~~> FTParams [ SameLine FTRets ] [ ";" ] ;

// A grouped type declaration (type ( A struct {...}; B int )): the balanced body is
// skipped and reported not implemented (its members are not built). Tried after
// TypeDecl, which needs an Id rather than '('.
TypeGroup   <~~ notImpl("grouped type declaration", up.pos) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd SkipGroup [ ";" ] ;

// const Name [Type] = expr is GENUINELY bound like a var (its value is reused). A
// grouped const block (const ( A = iota ... )) is skipped and reported not implemented
// (iota, implicit repetition and bit-shift expressions are not modeled).
// const ( A = iota; B; C ): every spec gets the running iota index, and a spec
// without its own '=' repeats the previous spec's expression list.
ConstGroup  <~~ push(makeConstGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd "(" { ConstSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
ConstSpec   <~~ push(makeConstSpec(cBindSpec(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = CSpecOn Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" CExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
ConstSingle <~~ push(makeVarDecl(cBindSpec(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd CSpecOn Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ VType ] [ "=" CExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
// The iota watermark. A const spec that mentions iota cannot be bound to ONE constant
// (its expression list is re-read once per spec with a different iota), so the count is
// taken here, before the spec's expressions, and compared again in cBindSpec.
CSpecOn     <~~ cIotaMark = cIotaCount ~~> = [ "\x00" ] ;
// An expression list that also tags each element's END POSITION - the key its folded
// constant was registered under, and the only way the binding can read it back.
CExprList   = CExprItem { "," CExprItem } ;
CExprItem   <~~ push({cat: up.pos}) ~~> = Expression ;

// func name(params) rets { } and func (r Type) name(params) rets { }
// The result list is optional, so like FuncTypeT's it has to be pinned to the line the
// parameters ended on: Go inserts a semicolon there, and `func (t T) m()` followed by
// `func main() {` otherwise reads the next declaration's `func` as this one's result
// type (NamedType's Id happily matches the keyword).
// A function may be declared WITHOUT a body: its implementation comes from assembly
// (or from another file the linker supplies), which this project can never provide.
// The syntax is accepted so the rest of the file parses; the missing body is reported
// as not implemented, so nobody mistakes the acceptance for support. The bodied form
// is tried first, so `func f() {` never reaches here.
FuncDecl    = FuncBodied | FuncNoBody ;
FuncBodied  <~~ buildFunc(takeAll()) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ SameLine RetTypes ] Block [ ";" ] ;
FuncNoBody  <~~ buildFunc(takeAll()) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ SameLine RetTypes ] NoBody [ ";" ] ;
// An always-empty match: Go source holds no NUL byte, so the optional never consumes
// anything and the production stands purely for its tag.
NoBody      <~~ push(notImplStmt("function without a body", up.pos)) ~~> = [ "\x00" ] ;
// Generics are ERASED: a type parameter list is parsed and ignored, the constraint with
// it, and an instantiation (f[int], T[K, V]) is the identity on the value it indexes.
// The value model is dynamically typed, so an erased generic behaves exactly like the
// instantiated one - only compile-time type CHECKING is lost, which a syntax-driven
// front end does not do anyway.
TypeParams  = "[" TypeParamGrp { "," TypeParamGrp } "]" ;
TypeParamGrp = Id { "," Id } Constraint ;
Constraint  = ConstrTerm { "|" ConstrTerm } ;
ConstrTerm  = [ "~" ] Type ;
// A POINTER receiver (func (c *T) m()) sees the caller's struct; a VALUE receiver
// (func (c T) m()) sees a copy, so mutations in the method do not reach the caller.
// A method may also leave the receiver UNNAMED (func (T) M(), func (*T) M()) when the
// body does not use it - very common in interface-satisfaction tests. Which shape it is
// is decided by a lookahead rather than by letting the named form try and fail, because
// `(T)` matches the named form's first Id and only dies at the second one. RecvAnon
// pushes an {anon} marker LAST (a tag runs after its children), and normRecv turns that
// into the canonical [name, {ptr}?, type] the method table expects, with an empty name.
Receiver    <~~ push({recv: normRecv(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "(" ( RecvNamed | RecvAnon ) ")" ;
RecvNamed   = RecvNamedAhead Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> [ "*" <~~ push({ptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ;
RecvAnon    <~~ push({anon: true}) ~~>
            = [ "*" <~~ push({ptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ;
// The receiver is NAMED exactly when an identifier is followed by another identifier
// or by '*' - `(p *T)` and `(p T)` are named, `(T)`, `(*T)` and `(T[P])` are not.
// A :script SUCCEEDS by returning undefined and FAILS by returning an impossible
// token, so the named case is the `undefined` one.
RecvNamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        while (i < 256) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        var isWord = (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128
        if (!isWord) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        while (i < 512) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        while (i < 768) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        if (cc === 42) { return undefined }
        if ((cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// Parameters: several names may share a type (a, b int), a name may be omitted
// entirely (func(int) - only in type positions), and the last one may be variadic
// (vals ...int). A named group is tried first; on an unnamed list it fails and the
// bare-type form takes over.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ ParamItem { "," ParamItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
ParamItem   = NamedParam | AnonParam ;
NamedParam  = Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> } [ Variadic ] PType ;
AnonParam   = [ Variadic ] PType ;
// The parameter's declared TYPE TEXT, captured exactly the way FType captures a
// struct field's. A parameter is an ordinary slot, so `func f(b uint8)` has to put
// the width on the argument on the way in - without this the b++ inside f stepped
// at 53 bits.
PType       <~~ push({pty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
Variadic    <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// Results: a single type, or a parenthesized list that may name its results
// (func f(a int) (q, r int)). Named results become zero-valued locals that a bare
// 'return' yields. NamedAhead decides which shape it is BEFORE any name is pushed,
// so the unnamed list never leaks half a name list onto the tag stack.
// THE RESULT TYPE TEXTS ARE CAPTURED, which they were not before, and two answers
// depended on it: a named result was declared holding the plain number 0 whatever its
// type (`func f() (r int, s string)` left s at 0, printing `0` where go prints the
// empty string), and a function whose panic was RECOVERED returned undefined - `<nil>`
// - where go returns the zero values of the declared result types. Both need the type
// text at the declaration, exactly as a PARAMETER already carries one through PType.
RetTypes    = NamedRets | RetOne | RetList ;
RetOne      <~~ push({rtys: cRty1(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
RetList     <~~ push({rtys: cRtyList(takeAll())}) ~~> = "(" [ RType { "," RType } ] ")" ;
RType       <~~ push({rty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
NamedRets   <~~ push(cNamedRets(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NamedAhead "(" NamedRetGrp { "," NamedRetGrp } ")" ;
NamedRetGrp = Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> } RType ;
// A parenthesized list is NAMED exactly when some element at depth 1 is two words
// separated by whitespace ('q, r int' has 'r int'; '(int, string)' has no such pair).
NamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var depth = 0
        var cc = 0
        var prevWord = false
        var sawSpace = false
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { break }
            var isWord = (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { sawSpace = true; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 40) { depth++; prevWord = false; sawSpace = false; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 41) {
                depth--
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                prevWord = false
                sawSpace = false
                i++
                continue
            }
            if (depth === 1 && prevWord && sawSpace && (isWord || cc === 91 || cc === 42)) { return undefined }
            prevWord = isWord
            sawSpace = false
            i++
        }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01unnamed-results", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A package level var, registered right away. Grouped (var ( a int; b = 2 )) runs each
// spec in order. A single const at top level is bound right away too.
TopVar      <~~ var t = pop(); t() ~~>
            = VarGroup | VarDecl ;
TopConst    <~~ var t = pop(); t() ~~>
            = ConstGroup | ConstSingle ;

Type        = "*" Type | "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT
            | StructTypeT | NamedType ;
// A named type: a predeclared name, a qualified one (time.Duration) or a generic
// instantiation (Stack[int]).
NamedType   = ( "int" KwEnd | "string" KwEnd | "bool" KwEnd | Id ) [ "." Id ] [ TypeArgs ] ;
TypeArgs    = "[" Type { "," Type } "]" ;
StructTypeT = "struct" KwEnd SkipBlock ;
// A pointer type (*T) is transparent (structs already behave like references) and a
// fixed size array ([N]T / [...]T) declares like a slice; the size is parsed, ignored.
ArrayTypeT  = "[" ( "..." | Expression <~~ pop() ~~> ) "]" Type ;
MapTypeT    = "map" KwEnd "[" Type "]" Type ;
// A func TYPE may name its parameters and its results exactly like a declaration does
// (type Seq[V any] func(yield func(V) bool)); the names are parsed and dropped, since a
// type carries no bindings. The shape mirrors ParamList/RetTypes, untagged - a Type has
// to stay item-neutral, so none of these productions may push.
// The result is optional, so it must not be allowed to run over the line end: Go
// inserts a semicolon after the ')' that ends the parameters when the line stops
// there, and without SameLine the optional result happily reads the NEXT top-level
// `func` declaration as this type's return type.
FuncTypeT   = "func" KwEnd FTParams [ SameLine FTRets ] ;
FTParams    = "(" [ FTItem { "," FTItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
FTItem      = FTNamed | FTAnon ;
FTNamed     = Id { "," Id } [ "..." ] Type ;
FTAnon      = [ "..." ] Type ;
FTRets      = FTRetNamed | Type | "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" ;
FTRetNamed  = NamedAhead "(" FTRetGrp { "," FTRetGrp } ")" ;
FTRetGrp    = Id { "," Id } Type ;
// Channel and (anonymous) interface types are parsed so declarations mentioning
// them type-check syntactically; their values are only produced/consumed through
// constructs that are themselves reported as not implemented.
ChanTypeT   = ( "chan" KwEnd [ "<-" ] | "<-" "chan" KwEnd ) Type ;
IfaceTypeT  = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   = Block | If | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Defer | GoStmt | Select | Fallthrough
            | TypeStmt | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | VarGroup | VarDecl | Labeled | SimpleStmt | Empty ;

// A type declaration in STATEMENT position (`func f() { type S []T; ... }`). Go scopes
// a local type to its block; the value model has one namespace for type names, so the
// declaration is built exactly as a top-level one, and the statement itself is a no-op
// (TypeDecl's own tag has already registered the descriptor by the time this runs).
TypeStmt    <~~ push(function() {}) ~~>
            = TypeDecl | TypeGroup ;

// fallthrough: accepted, reported not implemented (our switch has no fallthrough model).
// fallthrough: a signal the enclosing switch body catches (see runSwitch).
Fallthrough <~~ push(makeFallthrough()) ~~>
            = "fallthrough" KwEnd [ ";" ] ;

// go f(args) / go pkg.Fn(args): a goroutine. True concurrency is not modeled, so
// it is reported not implemented; under -warn-unsupported the call still evaluates
// (its callee stays visible in call graphs). select likewise has no channel runtime.
GoStmt      <~~ push(makeGoStmt(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "go" KwEnd PostfixExpr [ ";" ] ;
// select { case v := <-ch: ...; case ch <- x: ...; default: ... }. The arms are tried
// in source order and the first READY one runs, which under the cooperative model is
// deterministic. An arm's body is a statement list, so it needs NotSelLabel to stop
// before the next 'case' / 'default' (both would otherwise parse as an identifier
// expression, exactly as they would in a switch body - which is why that one is
// parsed flat instead).
Select      <~~ push(makeSelect(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "select" KwEnd "{" { SelArm } "}" ;
SelArm      = SelRecv | SelSend | SelDefault ;
SelRecv     <~~ push(makeSelRecv(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> ] ( ":=" | "=" ) ]
              "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelSend     <~~ push(makeSelSend(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd PostfixExpr "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelDefault  <~~ push(makeSelDefault(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "default" KwEnd ":" SelBody ;
SelBody     <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())); cScopeExit() ~~>
            = CScopeOn { SelStmt } ;
SelStmt     = NotSelLabel Statement ;
NotSelLabel = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        // Whitespace is not skipped before a guard, so step over it (and over line
        // comments) before looking at the keyword.
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01end-of-file", 0) }
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            if (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                while (i < 8192 && c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i++ }
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        var w = ""
        while (i < 8192 && w.length < 8) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 97 || cc > 122) { break }
            w += String.fromCharCode(cc)
            i++
        }
