// type. Go reads '(*X)(y)' as a conversion too, so this does not take anything away from
// a call through a parenthesized expression; '(*p).f' still falls through, because the
// '(' this rule needs after the type is a '.' there.
PtrTypeT    = "*" Type ;
// IfaceTypeT is here so that the UNPARENTHESIZED `interface{}(x)` is read as the
// conversion it is. `(interface{})(x)` already worked through ParenConvT and
// `var x interface{} = v` through the declaration, but the conversion EXPRESSION
// aborted - the interpreter with `unknown name: interface`, the compiler with
// `composite literal not implemented`, because CompLitNI's CompName matched the bare
